# Anger

*(A psychological story)*

Kuppam Subban was seized by fierce anger. The gruel his wife Kuppi had cooked was not ready; there was not even an onion to bite with it. Her answer, he complained, had neither salt nor savour. Must a man labour like an ox, return home, and eat dust? Her wits seemed to be worsening by the day.

Kuppi understood that these harsh words came from the weariness of his labour. The men of the settlement worked night and day only to raise their families. She resolved to discover what taste pleased him: ginger relish, lime juice, fried potatoes, yam curry, buttermilk gravy and vadai, field-bean mash, ladies' finger, drumstick, or tender brinjal. Yet could she ever discover the true cause of his bitterness?

Her dark body shone with labour and her ragged dress still revealed her youth. Kannappar, the wealthy owner of the mango grove, watched her run like a deer. Lust entered him. He told himself he must somehow possess her and arranged for her to work alone near his mansion. Kuppi gathered firewood, tied it into a bundle, and started home. Kannappar called her back and ordered her to carry it into a hut. When she returned, he stood waiting with a false smile.

She understood the danger. Anger blazed in her: should she trip him, shout, strike him with a stick, or stone him? The respected village elder, reputed to know truth and justice, now appeared as a predator of poor women. When he came close and touched her, she sprang away. There was one grasp, one shove, one startled cry. Kannappar fell, and Kuppi escaped.

For two days fear confused her. Kannappar had gone away to Kalugumalai. Could she tell her husband? Would he disbelieve her, blame her, or take a sickle and bring back the man's head? She remained silent. How could the gruel cook or an onion be found while such terror filled her mind? Kuppi's anger turned upon her father, who had refused to lend three hundred rupees with which the couple might buy a cow, sell milk and buttermilk, and avoid serving a sinner.

Subban's own anger had another, unknown cause. Kannappar had quarrelled over debt and litigation with Sokkan. Subban, finding Sokkan attacking him, struck Sokkan down with a single cut and ran home carrying the sickle. Kuppi, thinking he had killed the lust-maddened Kannappar, cried out in alarm. But Subban praised Kannappar as a man of gold and said he had killed the wicked Sokkan who attacked him.

What could Kuppi grieve over, and how much could her mind bear? Subban feared the police and spoke of fleeing far away. The court sentenced him to six years' rigorous imprisonment. When she visited, he consoled her: if she returned to the mango grove, the generous master would provide her gruel. Unable to reveal the truth or work there again, she said she would take a mill job in Madras. Subban grew furious, imagining that Kuppi had become unfaithful.

Each person nurses an anger of his or her own. But who will tell another the truth behind it?

*(Kanchi, 18 April 1965)*
